  "id": "Compound::DB00393 - Gene::779",
  "verbalization": "Nimodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is directly linked to the protein Cacna1s through a well-documented mechanism of action. The evidence suggests that Nimodipine, as part of the dihydropyridine class, binds to Cacna1s, which is involved in regulating calcium channels in cells. This binding is supported by multiple paths, with the lowest common ancestor (LCA) linking Nimodipine to Isradipine, a member of the dihydropyridine family, and both being classified under the broader category of calcium channel blockers. The LCA further links Nimodipine to Isopropyl Ester, reinforcing the biochemical relationship. The integration of these paths highlights a consistent mechanism of action, where Nimodipine exerts its effects by modulating calcium channels through its interaction with Cacna1s. The evidence is robust and aligns with established pharmacological principles, providing a clear and mechanistically sound pathway between Nimodipine and Cacna1s.",
